title: The operation log
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: overview
audience: dev
summary: The event log since October 2026: an ordered set of reversible operations grouped by nested labels, with one mechanism (revert onto the current state) instead of a history tree, HEAD navigation and rollback.

''Proposed 12 October 2026, built the following days.'' Until then the event log was a
tree of operations with a movable HEAD, two mechanisms (rollback and revert) and
revisions. It is now an ''ordered set of operations'' — why, in [[Why the log stops being a tree]] — built in steps: reverting onto the current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]); the log as a sequence, undo and redo as reverts; retention per origin
([[Automatic retention]]); labels instead of revisions, and contexts in the clients
([[Labels and contexts]]); undo and redo by label ([[Undo and redo by label]]); the log
panel as a tree of labels ([[log panel]]). Each step that changed the store's layout
''emptied the log'' of an older store rather than converting it — the metarecords kept —
which the user chose ([[Persisted format changes]]).

Two things were left for later, by the user's decision: the GUI's ''work context''
([[The GUI's work context]], built since, 17 October 2026), and ''"rollback" as "revert
everything since T"'' ([[Reverting everything since a time]]). Renaming or describing a
label after the fact is not built either.

!! The model

* The log is a sequence of operations, numbered in the order they were written. There is no parent, no HEAD to move, no branch: the log only grows, and shrinks only by retention.
* Each operation keeps what it found and what it wrote (the before/after snapshots of today). What it wrote is ''descriptive'': it says what the operation did, it is never a condition for undoing it.
* There is ''one mechanism'': reverting a set of operations, which applies each one's inverse to the ''current'' state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). Undo, redo, "back to this morning", a script's going back: each is a choice of set, nothing more. The name "rollback" may be reused for "revert everything since T".
* Operations are grouped by ''labels'' — named, described, nested — instead of revisions ([[Labels and contexts]]). A transaction stays one request; a label groups what belongs together, across as many requests as it takes.
* Undo and redo target a label of the newest operation, chosen by the user when there are several ([[Undo and redo by label]]).

!! Retention

With no dependency analysis left, any operation can go at any time without making another
one impossible to revert. Retention becomes a policy per origin: keep X operations of the
watcher, Y of everything else — so a user keeps a long history of their own changes while
the watcher's churn is trimmed. A label with no operation left goes with its last one.

The old "labelled revision is a retention floor" rule (doc "Automatic retention") is
dropped: naming a stretch of work is a context in the GUI ([[Labels and contexts]]), not
a promise to keep a global state that the log no longer knows how to rebuild.

!! What went

* HEAD's navigation, the history tree and its branches; LCA and navigation paths; `mode=active`/`mode=tree` on `GET /log`; `prune linearize`; `mf log list --tree`/`--graph` and the log panel's graph.
* The coordinated step-by-step navigation and its lock (`/rollback/*`, `mf log rollback`, `log:rollback`); the file side of a revert stays.
* The dependency analysis: `with_dependents`, "blocked by".
* Revisions, as a grouping and as a word: `rev_id`, `/log/revisions/:id`, labels set on a revision afterwards (`PATCH`, `log:mark`), the "labelled revision is a retention floor" rule.
* Restoring a past ''global'' state. Nothing in daily use needs it; the watcher's work makes it a state that never really existed on disk anyway.

!! Where it differs from the proposal

* ''Origin'' stays on each operation, set by the daemon, instead of being read off the root label a label descends from: a trashing's `trash` origin must survive inside any context ([[Operations and their origin]]).
* A command's label is opened ''at its first write'', the client sending a key (`X-Metafolder-Command`) rather than creating the label before writing — so a command that only reads opens nothing ([[Labels and contexts]]).
* A context names its repository (`METAFOLDER_LABEL=<repo>:<label>`): a label id means nothing in another one.
* The GUI knows which command runs, not which call each made: two commands running at once share a label.

